{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452539} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc68676c-ca1c-5219-8aa5-77122434c71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451385} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9ffda0-5e93-5ed9-aae6-cbb7e4cc5873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc68676c-ca1c-5219-8aa5-77122434c71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451385} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9acc5d1-05c8-538e-a571-a062b4fe74b6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00ac6a8e-9862-5276-9835-c156c0dea515" :question/math-academy-id "q-44058" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050810 :topic/id #uuid "6869b952-1056-567b-aee7-9b79143e8e5f" :topic/math-academy-id 2150 :topic/title "The Intermediate Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c7405ea-c98b-5325-b63e-2807e36d76d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467560} {:answer/id #uuid "26e98b7e-56f8-5b47-b85d-e433904b1fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26e98b7e-56f8-5b47-b85d-e433904b1fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467561} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2f07a7-b601-54ee-9407-23b9e2e1ccad" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468501} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd77ad93-91ef-5c0f-a754-61403fd2b082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467562} {:answer/id #uuid "3310ece2-aa6d-5452-bfaa-6e91380d4455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd77ad93-91ef-5c0f-a754-61403fd2b082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467562} :answer-field/id #uuid "66a80203-6b00-5a67-8ab9-e11f7f67970b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468502} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8378235-3099-5770-a4cf-2dbdf0e69e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467564} {:answer/id #uuid "f922edc2-68b0-5ca7-8e60-d2d9f9ab5036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8378235-3099-5770-a4cf-2dbdf0e69e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467564} :answer-field/id #uuid "3698a02e-a636-58f8-a9a1-b0ab2791efd5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "09a3da32-3de0-5834-befe-e2cd86cc2a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-47016" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to this function?\n- **I.** $[0,1]$\n- **II.** $[1,2]$\n- **III.** $[-1,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈1.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050810 :topic/id #uuid "6869b952-1056-567b-aee7-9b79143e8e5f" :topic/math-academy-id 2150 :topic/title "The Intermediate Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9571fe0-bef8-5f1e-9ba0-4c06a4b482c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467566} {:answer/id #uuid "4766a3dd-ffe7-58c7-97d9-8370199dc6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4766a3dd-ffe7-58c7-97d9-8370199dc6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467567} :answer-field/id #uuid "990578cb-68f3-5bff-ac55-4c4c35ea5506" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468504} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a11a4707-8927-586b-bc26-719ddc4f1372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467568} {:answer/id #uuid "47dbafb2-cabc-51dc-9055-c385426b609f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a11a4707-8927-586b-bc26-719ddc4f1372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467568} :answer-field/id #uuid "0126cb1a-d5b7-51f6-b9cd-d2b08e3e2f66" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468505} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "219820d5-c88c-55a4-9c0c-7ab18afb9233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467570} {:answer/id #uuid "55fd1b5c-e37f-51f0-b8af-339da920860d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "219820d5-c88c-55a4-9c0c-7ab18afb9233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467570} :answer-field/id #uuid "e605a49f-dffa-50b2-9558-b152728ee5ef" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fde248b-aec3-5f7a-8ce9-4a20cf4e8153" :question/math-academy-id "q-47035" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x(x+3)(x-2).$ On which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=-5$ has a solution?\n- **I.** $[-5,-3]$\n- **II.** $[-3,-2]$\n- **III.** $[-2,0]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is a polynomial, it's continuous over the real numbers. Thus, we can apply the intermediate value theorem to any of the given intervals.\n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-70$ | $0$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-5,-3],$ we have $f(-5)=-70$ and $f(-3)=0.$ Since $-5\\in [-70,0],$ the IVT does guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-3,-2],$ we have $f(-3)=0$ and $f(-2)=8.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,0],$ we have $f(-2)=8$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050810 :topic/id #uuid "6869b952-1056-567b-aee7-9b79143e8e5f" :topic/math-academy-id 2150 :topic/title "The Intermediate Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f344881-385c-5665-85eb-bf009723e051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467572} {:answer/id #uuid "b0338624-0f00-542b-ad9e-7189e7be0ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f344881-385c-5665-85eb-bf009723e051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467572} :answer-field/id #uuid "267d92af-d407-5c35-ac8f-a6c162abe01d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468507} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0eccd15-4643-594c-9b76-cc1f8b6669b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467574} {:answer/id #uuid "acfa8399-1619-5e81-b743-8ccb518c5111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acfa8399-1619-5e81-b743-8ccb518c5111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467575} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6b3564-dbb4-56c7-a3c8-0f7852ea53a6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468508} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddc250d-3035-5ac8-aff3-71357621fce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467576} {:answer/id #uuid "57d257e8-63d5-5cd3-990e-9061ae7ffc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ddc250d-3035-5ac8-aff3-71357621fce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467576} :answer-field/id #uuid "054c1693-cd72-59a0-8e2f-778669cf35f6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee052f2c-7f8b-5e1c-a710-1b1e4c2963cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-47055" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}.$ In which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=3$ has a solution? \n- **I.** $[-4,-1]$\n- **II.** $[3,5]$\n- **III.** $[-1,4]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=2,$ and so the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2$ \n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-4,-1],$ we have $f(-4)=0.5$ and $f(-1)=0.$ Since $3\\notin [0,0.5],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[3,5],$ we have $f(3)=4$ and $f(5)=2.$ Since $3\\in [2,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,4]$ contains the point $x=2.$ Therefore we can not apply the intermediate value theorem on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050359 :topic/id #uuid "424e5d7e-179f-525e-bdaa-70125807f572" :topic/math-academy-id 3563 :topic/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e62a7ed-740e-501b-8d6b-8c57af5873e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451387} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf62c5a-98bf-51e9-a149-c724db61f076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e62a7ed-740e-501b-8d6b-8c57af5873e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451387} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f43c87f-65a3-51ec-b164-06e6b244f523" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452541} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8154f840-2c2b-5e8c-9b2e-eb28ff7d2b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451389} {:answer/id #uuid "3e98e4ea-c043-58f5-8e9d-ec8d71f9fc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8154f840-2c2b-5e8c-9b2e-eb28ff7d2b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451389} :answer-field/id #uuid "22fbbc90-7f82-5a70-8099-fd93af7709a8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452542} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afaa458-53ac-5375-baf0-a40d0084b83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451391} {:answer/id #uuid "c86e299f-4afa-5a45-8a32-de03ce84947c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c86e299f-4afa-5a45-8a32-de03ce84947c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451392} :answer-field/id #uuid "62c54019-22ca-5af4-aaaf-df8830b76084" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ce070dc-f77b-529a-a508-4630f3613570" :question/math-academy-id "q-47455" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n\n- **II.** The function is increasing in the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\n- **III.** The function has no zeros." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/77/779d7a31646a229492a8165c8212a5c83ad7d37d843c1c1b3b36766a74bc5124.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=sec⁡x$ is decreasing on $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has no zeros."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d1fafe-67a4-5304-b42b-0fa34a95eaff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467578} {:answer/id #uuid "c76c9ea4-d5d0-5c09-adc5-db52b1568fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c76c9ea4-d5d0-5c09-adc5-db52b1568fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467579} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df948ff-c1ab-538f-b99e-ed07682677fc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6428bf6c-1cd8-523e-89a2-bbd6aefca97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467580} {:answer/id #uuid "49e7f113-867c-5ee0-a738-ba5b88651827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6428bf6c-1cd8-523e-89a2-bbd6aefca97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467580} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e8c77e6-a0b4-526b-bf8e-a435b7b66ac1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468511} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b22f319-2182-5f2f-984d-2c69a38c95bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467582} {:answer/id #uuid "f8184f79-583b-5721-92be-e461d3dc1a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b22f319-2182-5f2f-984d-2c69a38c95bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467582} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58f3fca-628f-5dd4-9837-348f91ad595e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c600e13-fdff-5526-82d0-6659ac10dfc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-47587" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ has no inverse\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=n$\n\nIII. All columns of $A$ are pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times$\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528296} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529204}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56e7c760-83ed-5281-9a05-02bdd53f472e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451393} {:answer/id #uuid "1c25083c-8b7e-5fe5-8f5e-8eb45804062d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c25083c-8b7e-5fe5-8f5e-8eb45804062d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451394} :answer-field/id #uuid "42b8cf12-b1ee-56df-a82b-c4c4b8dbd00b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "695aba89-3993-5a92-98dc-4e9a55e876f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451395} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e90fc7-716b-51e9-bf6e-6eb941d76661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "695aba89-3993-5a92-98dc-4e9a55e876f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451395} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e267111-0c10-5c40-af31-2927630381d9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452545} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eb458c5-d305-5c0f-b510-c154501be818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451397} {:answer/id #uuid "37efc5e7-9dbf-570d-856e-a6d8bc5960e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37efc5e7-9dbf-570d-856e-a6d8bc5960e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451398} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d6123ef-1037-5ebd-a9a2-fe6ad93895a1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5470569-5538-5c85-aec6-dfcb3a6e366a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47652" :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has no solution\n\n- **III.** The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6943c140-8ce8-5ef3-a24a-ec4dd3989d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467584} {:answer/id #uuid "f903da43-b955-557b-b07e-9a56a9cb9411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f903da43-b955-557b-b07e-9a56a9cb9411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467585} :answer-field/id #uuid "131a190d-2e93-5c45-8f99-0ed790058e01" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a3ed0f-3e98-54bf-9262-acf83b1ca310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467586} {:answer/id #uuid "ce418b35-cc2f-53d8-bacf-bc6a8f351d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce418b35-cc2f-53d8-bacf-bc6a8f351d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467587} :answer-field/id #uuid "71011c6f-48e1-5eb7-add1-32fab707cd37" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801e1932-e9a9-5a2e-a418-1e17c385bb76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467588} {:answer/id #uuid "6016d1b1-b380-5755-a999-dcec788a69fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "801e1932-e9a9-5a2e-a418-1e17c385bb76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467588} :answer-field/id #uuid "559e34e0-cb19-5844-a24f-bc87f0e1438e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffbb2a4c-595d-59cc-bffd-a39a18b105b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-47657" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the unique solution of $Ax=0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ .*\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $A$ is invertible\n- **III.** ${A}^{-1}$ does not exist\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements:\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $det(A)\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $A$ is invertible .*$\\,✓$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420 :topic/id #uuid "9d6972e8-70d1-541f-8fd7-7896e8369046" :topic/math-academy-id 1691 :topic/title "Continuity and Differentiability of Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76eec529-1ff7-57ad-8dc7-b9e3a7ca7314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/va