nswer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59f4be26-745f-500a-8014-e08a471c0a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451427} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a523b7-faa6-5772-a250-4264622204cb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f25b7dd1-f51b-52ea-a0c4-08f9ed823c0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47747" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Its domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty $\n- **II.** It's a decreasing function\n- **III.** $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/28/288adf3d1da5cf4bb6e2530318aeb083b69d43639de7704f9df15672be477d0a.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc37e5f-628a-5453-b9aa-5f7736bbc9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467590} {:answer/id #uuid "86c33542-1a37-5a8c-829b-f0ce4c00b10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cc37e5f-628a-5453-b9aa-5f7736bbc9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467590} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c6904f-d234-579f-a1ce-7aceea241e6b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468516} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b766daf9-f662-5b41-bf41-146b6ff13df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467592} {:answer/id #uuid "ffeccce2-717e-5e80-b3c0-db1259995cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffeccce2-717e-5e80-b3c0-db1259995cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467593} :answer-field/id #uuid "814f8eea-219e-55ac-a376-98a938bdbd22" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468517} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7babf862-7892-5b69-9b8c-2026a9d0a99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467594} {:answer/id #uuid "61ed1c67-3d18-527d-aa8b-5e81455211be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7babf862-7892-5b69-9b8c-2026a9d0a99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467594} :answer-field/id #uuid "95cc6ec8-9bed-5ab7-9e0d-2ce973417c1a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a410e5f1-db4f-5d99-8a2f-cd16595bdd74" :question/math-academy-id "q-47923" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $[0.5,1]$\n- **III.** $[-1,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"} :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508384} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509010}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3a12c7-a73a-5bc2-907f-c5743849d466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467596} {:answer/id #uuid "1091a2ee-d878-5986-8a1d-8dce0d8d151f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab3a12c7-a73a-5bc2-907f-c5743849d466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467596} :answer-field/id #uuid "5559424d-c194-5562-a944-389f6915fbf5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468519} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f82f28d2-0885-5f2b-a2b4-b8de495aa401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467598} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e9e066-f343-5582-805e-117abcb6788e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f82f28d2-0885-5f2b-a2b4-b8de495aa401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467598} :answer-field/id #uuid "9679911f-d61d-52a1-a7c3-1da4986dee9c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468520} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f8bfe43-a15d-5668-9257-74a19adfbbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467600} {:answer/id #uuid "bdbfb014-4e69-5922-98b2-654a079851f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdbfb014-4e69-5922-98b2-654a079851f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467601} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebca5b66-72d2-5051-b663-b25f725bdc65" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "632c55e7-35c1-5a02-8973-af67647c4724" :question/math-academy-id "q-48018" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡(x-2)?$  \n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $[2,3)$\n- **III.** $[3,4]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $y={log}_{2}⁡(x-2)$ is not defined anywhere in this interval. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[2,3)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[3,4]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[3,4].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3f46a85-bb8a-511d-ad66-524153b56e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467602} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac808f8-b76f-5da9-9c2c-f50846ef33e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ac808f8-b76f-5da9-9c2c-f50846ef33e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467603} :answer-field/id #uuid "69cd4ca1-53f7-5c26-b39d-e11af89e4852" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468522} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "867861da-42a3-56d6-b2be-e893dd3a6ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467604} {:answer/id