box} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a1259cd-784d-5b09-bc57-e1c65f1d431f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70768" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n- **I.** $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- **II.** $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$\n- **III.** $(-1,\\pi )$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\pi $ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\pi )$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048794 :topic/id #uuid "bf6b1d4a-a2a5-5f59-9697-5ed210454e3e" :topic/math-academy-id 1486 :topic/title "Graphing the Inverse Cosine Function"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0764b808-2439-5977-abc9-4df3e6e27d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467824} {:answer/id #uuid "1106e80c-03ae-5a78-95c0-228a6df04897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0764b808-2439-5977-abc9-4df3e6e27d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467824} :answer-field/id #uuid "83870a56-ad42-5be3-ac02-d6017dfa6550" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468633} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6549a469-9116-5436-8269-73fd9e8f8acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467826} {:answer/id #uuid "f35242b6-9bcc-58df-8ac4-9f91fa605200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6549a469-9116-5436-8269-73fd9e8f8acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467826} :answer-field/id #uuid "75580c66-99eb-5cde-9e85-adda8e2ec87e" :an