e graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110587 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b834b64-9679-56c2-b5e9-ee26a54b5e13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Tangent Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049656 :topic/id #uuid "08bc988d-9fd4-51b9-b9a1-01573e3679f4" :topic/math-academy-id 1487 :topic/title "Graphing the Inverse Tangent Function"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a2d9a9-577b-5444-9d1c-92bf0a7facff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467830} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb1cc8e-f502-5f32-9bac-9ac87104593e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #