} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eeedf4d-8de8-56c0-94c3-b7f2c06a9024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468050} {:answer/id #uuid "401e04fe-e906-51ad-8dfa-8a0c0aebd6fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eeedf4d-8de8-56c0-94c3-b7f2c06a9024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468050} :answer-field/id #uuid "e04dd868-99c1-5128-856f-dcf493097c6c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1eaad6e-884d-57bd-a4ae-a690d3d6abe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73499" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1), & x>1 \\\\ x-1, & x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[0,2]$\n\nII. $[-1,0]$\n\nIII. $[-1,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08258f8b-dcaa-5fc6-ae65-f632b2ec32c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468052} {:answer/id #uuid "29791dee-07cd-517e-ade1-ff8025ea2d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08258f8b-dcaa-5fc6-ae65-f632b2ec32c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468052} :answer-field/id #uuid "e380e8d8-76cf-5f78-bbc9-d5f2bfff507e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fa6caaf-0d53-550a-81a3-65d8c87e7671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468054} {:answer/id #uuid "7b92b7c9-5e55-5053-bc30-ae2124f51f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fa6caaf-0d53-550a-81a3-65d8c87e7671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468054} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7306f5-05ce-59d5-926c-66b49d14119f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ffb37d-c85f-5070-8a42-00349cde98df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468056} {:answer/id #uuid "332ea8dc-cd67-5380-9b7b-ad3430d95bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "332ea8dc-cd67-5380-9b7b-ad3430d95bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468057} :answer-field/id #uuid "3acf4f19-1ed8-5841-bd74-4e01d898c416" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a761269-f6ea-50eb-8e45-58983c3d9692" :question/math-academy-id "q-73503" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $(0,3]$\n- **III.** $[-2,-1]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"} :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508384} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509024}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8c0277-f286-5f5c-95f1-742c5c84a4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468058} {:answer/id #uuid "be8dd074-7787-59f7-a72c-bcaee5b2f576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8c0277-f286-5f5c-95f1-742c5c84a4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468058} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5514612-b769-5808-8bd5-f23974490b11" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468750} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61a803aa-7689-5e3d-ac70-2fd7924783b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468060} {:answer/id #uuid "54a9b0c7-fc2a-5a6c-8b61-d9b8cb0d6b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61a803aa-7689-5e3d-ac70-2fd7924783b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468060} :answer-field/id #uuid "796ce61b-6691-5177-9e8d-07206e5a1e77" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc0fda9f-032f-5239-88db-2a5cd71a0cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468062} {:answer/id #uuid "5592e116-d543-53b7-bc8a-6200d16d248a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5592e116-d543-53b7-bc8a-6200d16d248a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468063} :answer-field/id #uuid "0afadab0-2a76-5ed8-839b-7a13c2067a7a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcdd6cca-dc15-5e0c-a55e-4d73a59866ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-73504" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-1, & x<1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡(x-1), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,2]$\n- **II.** $(1,3]$\n- **III.** $[2,\\pi ]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "590b6563-fc5a-5099-8b31-7c8840139fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468064} {:answer/id #uuid "823fa8bb-4902-59a1-8b15-a420ef200eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "590b6563-fc5a-5099-8b31-7c8840139fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468064} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03606bc-af56-5495-a640-2b1ff321627a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468753} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6551ac70-7210-595b-b7c9-6d9837da871d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468066} {:answer/id #uuid "cad2d236-f9c3-5706-b2e8-53826efc2a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6551ac70-7210-595b-b7c9-6d9837da871d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468066} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a52ec7c-0e75-5e34-b576-a534c8cf21a1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44dc5099-0725-53e3-b405-c81f11d46f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468068} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7e8804-4489-51c0-b955-8a411b43aaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b7e8804-4489-51c0-b955-8a411b43aaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468069} :answer-field/id #uuid "e79dce1b-5f18-5741-977a-3edf1f823ef0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8abc4b9f-caf8-5c94-91da-62ad0ae61b3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73531" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-2,2]$\n\nII. $[-3,1)$\n\nIII. $[1.5,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-2,2].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\n- Finally consider  $[1.5,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval  $[1.5,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[1.5,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979 :topic/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c94e30f0-68f2-5998-b9f9-644e9e44b95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468070} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0ed006-c485-5f47-9cb3-66d73cb5b6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c94e30f0-68f2-5998-b9f9-644e9e44b95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468070} :answer-field/id #uuid "e579a2ca-789a-528f-993a-72fedc592395" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5794e6f7-e7be-5b7e-8bb8-bb34003ae429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468072} {:answer/id #uuid "c1777e06-539e-50b4-a94c-4208586ed294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5794e6f7-e7be-5b7e-8bb8-bb34003ae429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468072} :answer-field/id #uuid "3694904e-0ccc-5a31-b8a9-6ec58e4eec46" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468757} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6117a2a3-4603-5502-8e5e-e303e66ecd3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468074} {:answer/id #uuid "32f5d350-119e-5636-b3cb-0f999c5e3d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32f5d350-119e-5636-b3cb-0f999c5e3d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468075} :answer-field/id #uuid "abcdcfba-e886-55b6-abf8-403802e888af" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "451e02c3-10e2-5101-a83f-538a8be6b03d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73549" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the ellipses $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is a type I region\n- **II.** It is a type II region\n- **III.** It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/62/627e7e102a0f16cb0c4946e42b48ad122299d729c5bc419293e258a3f2e1701e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.25$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/d7/d7aa48c887d6fb1c3d0420867b7e74010fa93e16d7e24317e2cf214764e56113.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/ec/ec632fa5f86d8d9b34b9ccf661a3219e965f289c28bf69d7d80eeeb46093e7ae.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979 :topic/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space"} :activity/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514859} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515626}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb2f9c1-c0f7-5cb1-94cf-c1eda0965c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451585} {:answer/id #uuid "fb399ecd-7930-593b-83c0-38b52228b9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb399ecd-7930-593b-83c0-38b52228b9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451586} :answer-field/id #uuid "a104f9c7-7e4d-5227-b5d9-cb72664cf694" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ec2e54f-4842-5a73-9901-8fa041c1a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451587} {:answer/id #uuid "7460347d-b166-5979-bcfc-69ae5393c4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ec2e54f-4842-5a73-9901-8fa041c1a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451587} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdfa12c5-bb59-5c70-9e62-1ffe6ff85434" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452641} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f7cb3cb-e223-5f6f-b2d5-80b3571e9e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451589} {:answer/id #uuid "5475d51e-e019-56ab-8cdc-1e208b5f796a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f7cb3cb-e223-5f6f-b2d5-80b3571e9e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451589} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6537431-c15f-5384-9d0f-e746bb1bc7d8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6905816-c0d6-59f0-9ff1-518732f97672" :question/math-academy-id "q-73562" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $A$ is not invertible\n- **III.** The system $Ax=b$ has no solution for some $2\\times 1$ column vectors $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57f8db18-4936-5d58-b7eb-8e4f05c46d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451591} {:answer/id #uuid "c921bd68-ad9c-592c-9800-47bf22685ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57f8db18-4936-5d58-b7eb-8e4f05c46d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451591} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e23e151-69ab-5150-a6dd-dddffad11cdb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452643} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67e5e15f-8163-5919-96fd-e0ad4f34643e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451593} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b68a52-378f-5960-b347-47f6ca687d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b68a52-378f-5960-b347-47f6ca687d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451594} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa3ed8d-d8dc-50ef-a617-8f9288b9d4b0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5854a3-4400-582b-bf8c-6ff607f43d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451595} {:answer/id #uuid "c1601bb5-6b85-53dc-a4db-7a5cfa40df0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1601bb5-6b85-53dc-a4db-7a5cfa40df0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451596} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9234a48-131e-5b5b-beae-659f6d2f37fe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12c8cd48-ec61-5529-9409-02950b9c8c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-73563" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** The system $Ax=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution\n- **III.** $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements II  and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60635149-f2da-567f-9f0a-9d4117894692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451597} {:answer/id #uuid "d830d804-449e-5e28-85c1-6748faf8d484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d830d804-449e-5e28-85c1-6748faf8d484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451598} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e139a64-7bda-5908-828b-d778f0b7810e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58ffdb3c-5c54-5de6-884b-cb4ea0bf2b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451599} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f9328e-0820-5a7e-a411-86187b35cbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58ffdb3c-5c54-5de6-884b-cb4ea0bf2b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451599} :answer-field/id #uuid "358dbeef-add1-5d3c-974e-66d81b7957e3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452647} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8c76fc4-db40-5d9f-b744-f4e8311ffe23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451601} {:answer/id #uuid "669f7e2b-36da-5c77-9bf8-b929a0f8fb6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669f7e2b-36da-5c77-9bf8-b929a0f8fb6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451602} :answer-field/id #uuid "3286acfd-ad75-5a43-83b7-7026982ba30d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b4e1216-e41e-5317-bf20-2acb3db54b81" :question/math-academy-id "q-73565" :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n\n- **II.** The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n\n- **III.** ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement II is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e722ed3-73e4-52c0-a421-554b9e9509ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451603} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8c1a33-14d5-5cc1-8cc8-cfa132ee5905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e722ed3-73e4-52c0-a421-554b9e9509ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451603} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f67ecc6-4e68-5ff5-857f-036ed453fead" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6115e85e-49af-5de4-9017-fbc84811fcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451605} {:answer/id #uuid "eaebde56-3eac-5bbe-8241-b7a00be555c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6115e85e-49af-5de4-9017-fbc84811fcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451605} :answer-field/id #uuid "9389fb0c-e8ff-5dd4-bcab-a96185abbeb4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452650} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa797b64-c1e0-5eb2-85fb-866fba1ac56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451607} {:answer/id #uuid "896ae4a2-9a84-5c01-a7e8-af08641f9223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "896ae4a2-9a84-5c01-a7e8-af08641f9223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451608} :answer-field/id #uuid "39b24a35-e5b9-5f93-a09d-4ba2dac53cd5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "946ddb30-e9a3-52e2-ad46-5aa2c1f15eef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73567" :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** The lines $2x+2y=2$ and $3x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n- **III.** $Ax=0$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2k-6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- Statement III is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statement III is true." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494315} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495228}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f23fb601-4602-5230-a2b6-d601d28b1d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451609} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8e66cf-8fa0-5d72-a062-c894bd90eeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc8e66cf-8fa0-5d72-a062-c894bd90eeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451610} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8a8b707-5ade-50d0-9352-ee18ad208d90" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4599a9d8-85d9-55cd-b0ce-93297e11ff44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451611} {:answer/id #uuid "8805138a-b896-57c2-ae9a-7c7b7fcdcb7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4599a9d8-85d9-55cd-b0ce-93297e11ff44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451611} :answer-field/id #uuid "261382cf-81eb-57f1-8fd5-08c17079a626" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452653} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f312ba01-7f4f-5870-a564-3e53f73eba37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451613} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd13e80-555a-5431-b4aa-3bbfe2eec5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f312ba01-7f4f-5870-a564-3e53f73eba37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451613} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa9d9259-e497-5632-a846-f4d0b61339a2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb595552-3588-5923-aa25-072e92fab82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73570" :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** The system $Ax=b$ has infinitely many solutions\n- **III.** The lines $x+2y=2$ and $4x+ky=0$ don't intersect" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k-8\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 8.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false.  According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049045 :topic/id #uuid "d599481a-4f0d-50b9-8ceb-db8805f8eeee" :topic/math-academy-id 2094 :topic/title "The Dot Product in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0b1c4a0-5efb-57ea-afe1-d4dc0d263d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451615} {:answer/id #uuid "aea40534-564b-5041-9106-8f422cba5401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0b1c4a0-5efb-57ea-afe1-d4dc0d263d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451615} :answer-field/id #uuid "17efcf99-d195-5449-b270-dd763616532f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452655} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298b65b7-d917-54c3-8430-6e1c198fd17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451617} {:answer/id #uuid "843c4f82-ee34-5415-8ec5-3eb2c9d21dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "298b65b7-d917-54c3-8430-6e1c198fd17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451617} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb97538-a4a6-59cb-ba0c-18441a2d3fcd" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100f83a6-d105-585a-835c-7c9b9b59b8c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451619} {:answer/id #uuid "a78b485d-bb5b-5f50-9378-ad835cb44037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a78b485d-bb5b-5f50-9378-ad835cb44037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451620} :answer-field/id #uuid "2337796b-4d0f-559b-8850-eb51b2d663f4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec2dfaf1-eafb-5d88-8d0f-be9d2909829b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74049" :question/problem "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $a\\cdot a<0$\n- **II.** $b\\cdot a=a\\cdot a$\n- **III.** $b\\cdot a<0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $a\\ne 0$, we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049045 :topic/id #uuid "d599481a-4f0d-50b9-8ceb-db8805f8eeee" :topic/math-academy-id 2094 :topic/title "The Dot Product in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d84f96c-b8a2-51f6-9858-6d35b74de32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451621} {:answer/id #uuid "2d44e4e0-9af3-583b-a878-ab37a166cbd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d84f96c-b8a2-51f6-9858-6d35b74de32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451621} :answer-field/id #uuid "3191aba1-8c3b-544c-a448-7745b8dfff89" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a247f262-d6e4-5450-a67b-170bc47e81aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451623} {:answer/id #uuid "17f28d17-67d0-5dc2-8fe6-3279cee00187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a247f262-d6e4-5450-a67b-170bc47e81aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451623} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cf96421-3521-5d61-8614-44b48da5a736" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452659} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51844bab-271c-5019-b872-1cab5ca37a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451625} {:answer/id #uuid "c0622aed-18df-5638-9aa1-8074a5ea611e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0622aed-18df-5638-9aa1-8074a5ea611e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451626} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c5252e3-564e-590c-a019-c205c05e4034" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "576bb373-56a3-5da0-ab43-2d02b000f274" :question/math-academy-id "q-74051" :question/problem "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $a\\cdot a=0$\n\n- **II.** $a\\cdot b>0$\n\n- **III.** $a\\cdot b<0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $a\\ne 0$, we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot b & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445 :topic/id #uuid "a01c36d3-5643-5685-845e-29c37e89238f" :topic/math-academy-id 1733 :topic/title "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08768d96-6543-5057-b351-a7b1e161594f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438520} {:answer/id #uuid "e55197f9-beea-5d56-b568-4686a6fafa27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08768d96-6543-5057-b351-a7b1e161594f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438520} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c63157-7a5a-5373-8ad8-5fe787c42154" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439289} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e975cf8-6ced-5fcc-a167-0751d7c6d489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438522} {:answer/id #uuid "6a5469f6-81d5-5ff6-93db-790de506e3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e975cf8-6ced-5fcc-a167-0751d7c6d489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438522} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9e9ab2-76ef-5705-8ab7-acb52a0677b5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439290} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2583daa7-3471-5757-b97e-74aae9210e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438524} {:answer/id #uuid "56894365-ac99-5761-9fdc-76740ad849bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56894365-ac99-5761-9fdc-76740ad849bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438525} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd6825c7-6c00-5310-b8fa-2fe4fbc48f05" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edc80b1b-d469-5071-b24d-3c90bb47fce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74635" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+4y+3z &=0 \\\\ x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system has no solution\n- **II.** The system is consistent independent\n- **III.** The system is consistent dependent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means that  we can find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445 :topic/id #uuid "a01c36d3-5643-5685-845e-29c37e89238f" :topic/math-academy-id 1733 :topic/title "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d964555-6f83-5191-89db-fd72ffdf14c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438526} {:answer/id #uuid "ad751ae7-e25b-5406-b9e3-27ea4cbed2f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d964555-6f83-5191-89db-fd72ffdf14c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438526} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1a0230-4005-55bc-8174-e0944a31dd3e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439292} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be3c581-4293-5cdb-b090-f3c480d1c024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438528} {:answer/id #uuid "89e07487-bb39-5368-bf53-e4b20abb5d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89e07487-bb39-5368-bf53-e4b20abb5d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438529} :answer-field/id #uuid "d62a54fe-bb91-5a39-8c45-d4f5ab79b9a1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d848cbc-71b2-5e07-ad3c-93e27231b7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438530} {:answer/id #uuid "82fdc104-5ebd-5054-99d2-64d287c3ea35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d848cbc-71b2-5e07-ad3c-93e27231b7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438530} :answer-field/id #uuid "a883d2db-1de6-5620-afb1-bd3d06ab629d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0089d19-a4b5-5ec4-beaa-917a294458ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-74637" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=-1 \\\\ x-2y &=1 \\\\ -x-5y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system has no solution\n- **II.** The system is consistent independent\n- **III.** The system is consistent dependent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=1 \\\\ 7y &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-\\frac{3}{7}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-\\frac{3}{7})=1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\n \nSo, we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931 :topic/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f3e9a8-09a1-5f57-86fa-04e7b6915e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468094} {:answer/id #uuid "78fcd3c1-4a11-58fd-8ce6-df88fc49f25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78fcd3c1-4a11-58fd-8ce6-df88fc49f25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468095} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c6cf87b-5ae8-56c1-a044-0835a6c8cde4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468768} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311f4a3b-6073-5441-be15-0e0c7cc774ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468096} {:answer/id #uuid "88d6ad28-2d86-517c-95bc-f86d6c74568c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88d6ad28-2d86-517c-95bc-f86d6c74568c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468097} :answer-field/id #uuid "d491b47c-a016-53fb-a14f-bea3b0d37235" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f46b92-20fa-5e06-82d5-4d57f3b2567e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468098} {:answer/id #uuid "5c70d63f-3608-5e38-a9a1-5aca4eef4730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5f46b92-20fa-5e06-82d5-4d57f3b2567e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468098} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0d14fb0-548c-5014-9418-0de578d5c27b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5fe00a2-ab56-5281-b3c2-6fe8dad2417a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74964" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- **II.** The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- **III.** The limit exists and is equal to $2$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743 :topic/id #uuid "ba8c9515-59e3-55c9-9d86-1de6e55c5474" :topic/math-academy-id 1779 :topic/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aff888b-3abd-519a-b020-976dc34f4245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468100} {:answer/id #uuid "25a7ba29-bc65-581b-9964-6c11c222d79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aff888b-3abd-519a-b020-976dc34f4245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468100} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f47bd49-5b77-5103-8d2d-487667211ef5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cbf7e8b-34e0-5ba3-bf96-0aa8c395b97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468102} {:answer/id #uuid "a74eb240-f723-5f61-8cdf-f327609c7cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cbf7e8b-34e0-5ba3-bf96-0aa8c395b97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468102} :answer-field/id #uuid "1002dbbd-d4c2-58a4-9d7a-a603394b05ab" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468772} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323a7811-4035-5802-b65f-fa37ee80f272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468104} {:answer/id #uuid "6196d8dc-3375-5b48-89b1-f58fc5a8344c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6196d8dc-3375-5b48-89b1-f58fc5a8344c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468105} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c3ed68e-ffbd-5c3c-9b32-90b080f28a48" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89a636cd-a216-5d73-bd3f-7f9cbe4badf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-75116" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n- **I.** $C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **III.** $C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{L}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}^{U}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form  because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743 :topic/id #uuid "ba8c9515-59e3-55c9-9d86-1de6e55c5474" :topic/math-academy-id 1779 :topic/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e49e90f-76ff-5359-a83b-d19ac304cc98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468106} {:answer/id #uuid "10dda677-3970-5dca-925d-f1fd92be396a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e49e90f-76ff-5359-a83b-d19ac304cc98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468106} :answer-field/id #uuid "c64d7a54-ae7c-59e3-b98f-bb2beec3e574" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0861ba46-156e-530e-b250-d70406ef7823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468108} {:answer/id #uuid "2ceee5b2-6950-50cb-8401-965ea76654fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0861ba46-156e-530e-b250-d70406ef7823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468108} :answer-field/id #uuid "2077df0d-33ac-5ccf-9556-c6e7cc2502c2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ff15f0-f00d-52b8-b7dc-4e1b5d1b9bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468110} {:answer/id #uuid "c91cd91b-5643-5712-a070-3652a02db457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c91cd91b-5643-5712-a070-3652a02db457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468111} :answer-field/id #uuid "0accbf5a-ff99-5165-88e6-f3ee36af91b4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0305a43d-bf81-5ecb-8a77-49a30ade0147" :question/math-academy-id "q-75117" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:  \n\n$$\nA=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{L}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}^{U}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$  on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal. \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743 :topic/id #uuid "ba8c9515-59e3-55c9-9d86-1de6e55c5474" :topic/math-academy-id 1779 :topic/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices"}]}] 