nswer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f25df3d-4b0c-5b67-901d-8d878d2769ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-75655" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- **II.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- **III.** The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/id #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1861 :topic/title "Linear Dependence and Independence"} :activity/title "Linear Dependence and Independence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572303} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572786}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/id #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1861 :topic/title "Linear Dependence and Independence"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bafe8fd-72d8-57d1-bc5f-2d080ed75be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468154} {:answer/id #uuid "85d07ff4-4f7d-5633-83c1-35a840fa221c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bafe8fd-72d8-57d1-bc5f-2d080ed75be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468154} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6d4ee9-771a-5696-bc88-03f95f6a365b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468798} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0211896a-1507-5fae-b661-63d9b4006bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468156} {:answer/id #uuid "e538415b-f7c4-5194-8711-1b826788fa1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e538415b-f7c4-5194-8711-1b826788fa1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468157} :answer-field/id #uuid "76ccb41d-e467-5e5a-8866-b19917b240b8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468799} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "617d8744-96d7-5762-8987-dbfc8acaed28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468158} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a4a05a-b344-5855-b641-f345041b157b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2a4a05a-b344-5855-b641-f345041b157b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468159} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2aa78d3-df8a-59d9-9a6e-298d4dfe1189" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d48ab70-ab46-5142-a432-382f1feb7bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75659" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/id #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1861 :topic/title "Linear Dependence and Independence"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493d511c-3fa2-54f4-906f-3d10df060915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468160} {:answer/id #uuid "8f671f0b-993d-528f-b2eb-6b89c5eb4f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f671f0b-993d-528f-b2eb-6b89c5eb4f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468161} :answer-field/id #uuid "984e11bf-4e99-50cb-a93f-68d5978b7a68" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468801} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0174516-2301-5678-aabd-d794fd74401e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468162} {:answer/id #uuid "426af5ad-f62d-552a-9782-a9d6d03d7b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0174516-2301-5678-aabd-d794fd74401e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468162} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ee08d3-8d95-51aa-87d4-23a80c7b3d51" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468802} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "392d404a-6778-5ca0-8617-cce66facc8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468164} {:answer/id #uuid "72c49299-2a0c-575b-8373-00f8a3aee6a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "392d404a-6778-5ca0-8617-cce66facc8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468164} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a289ad7-4d94-5940-ba11-7c8a3c9465d3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d177800-f6a6-59b3-bf6e-5adc772e358a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75660" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+13{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 13 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050122 :topic/id #uuid "2eaec2b9-4c03-5efd-b5aa-8e9655cca4f4" :topic/math-academy-id 1724 :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5b4b6c9-b120-5669-8778-9d44bd03a705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451627} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9ff174-54f1-5fc0-82c9-56c7f68be83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c9ff174-54f1-5fc0-82c9-56c7f68be83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451628} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6596886-eb35-5a15-8678-0d03898ac57c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452661} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b1b7dcd-0a45-5a31-b1e7-359ba6b8ce48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451629} {:answer/id #uuid "2844641e-66b1-56cc-8371-e36f6a1b3ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b1b7dcd-0a45-5a31-b1e7-359ba6b8ce48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451629} :answer-field/id #uuid "648f05bf-a002-5add-8c9a-e67860f117ba" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aec13ecc-22bc-5436-b215-dddd0d181f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451631} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfc103f-6039-5ed8-b224-6f15716dcaa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aec13ecc-22bc-5436-b215-dddd0d181f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451631} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce1dbe9-397b-565f-a4c9-e832cb32d032" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1c88851c-8567-55d2-897e-8b0f9b020c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-75662" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A(B+C)=AC+AB$\n- **II.** $A(BC)=(BA)C$\n- **III.** $AB=BA$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\nA(B+C)=AB+AC=AC+AB\n$$\n\n since matrix addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC) &=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050122 :topic/id #uuid "2eaec2b9-4c03-5efd-b5aa-8e9655cca4f4" :topic/math-academy-id 1724 :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fd2d4af-fcc8-55b6-bf9b-9feed35a4e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451633} {:answer/id #uuid "8c44c3d1-8408-5b27-9d56-5820850d8e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fd2d4af-fcc8-55b6-bf9b-9feed35a4e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451633} :answer-field/id #uuid "875098b9-5492-5e1a-b53e-55972f8d78c0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c09b308-44c0-5d82-b117-e79d490fc908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451635} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ea9fe4-7d06-5ee2-81e0-6e343876dfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5ea9fe4-7d06-5ee2-81e0-6e343876dfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451636} :answer-field/id #uuid "f365105a-7265-5164-bb84-ae009541d7d4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452665} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086cedd9-2c5d-5cf5-8342-fff602804217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451637} {:answer/id #uuid "d576ec60-2dbd-5562-90ac-ff700e520b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d576ec60-2dbd-5562-90ac-ff700e520b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451638} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf272d3-96d9-5e52-bf3a-2077d76af75c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8619c3de-3523-5f87-a248-dac65df502b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-75664" :question/problem "Given that $A$ and $B$  are $4\\times 4$ matrices,  and ${I}_{4}$ is the $4\\times 4$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA$\n- **II.** $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A$\n- **III.** $A(B+{I}_{4})=AB+A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A=A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+{I}_{4})=AB+A{I}_{4}=AB+A$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II  and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050122 :topic/id #uuid "2eaec2b9-4c03-5efd-b5aa-8e9655cca4f4" :topic/math-academy-id 1724 :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d29adab0-49f5-5be8-9dfb-41b652b9f78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451639} {:answer/id #uuid "aae53705-b1e7-5ad4-a458-fc12bd5715e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d29adab0-49f5-5be8-9dfb-41b652b9f78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451639} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec2f6a3-b7c4-5dbf-b313-e1460364d497" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "163312b5-47ae-51a4-8302-780627f27eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451641} {:answer/id #uuid "8391eb9d-de48-5257-958c-71e626ee3926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "163312b5-47ae-51a4-8302-780627f27eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451641} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e9e4ab6-972e-5a28-8a06-204e593f15c3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452668} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5abcda23-f4db-5653-8749-2127e4eb2cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451643} {:answer/id #uuid "83789ace-8be4-5fa7-bb78-667b324a5f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83789ace-8be4-5fa7-bb78-667b324a5f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451644} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebdef35b-4dc7-5168-954f-85f588629353" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ca7b9cd-e052-55a9-b9b9-9f438a40909d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75717" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $CB=BC$\n- **II.** $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- **III.** $A(BC)=(AB)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $A(BC)=(AB)C$ is always true.\n\nTherefore, only statement  III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819 :topic/id #uuid "c29ac9c3-f2c1-572b-9251-f82101c5a30e" :topic/math-academy-id 1375 :topic/title "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fba09f7-6fb0-5d6c-8424-e721e8481780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468166} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfcaae9-18c3-556e-876b-dbe83dd13637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fba09f7-6fb0-5d6c-8424-e721e8481780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468166} :answer-field/id #uuid "1111e0c2-88ed-5b55-afd9-e459c3ebe34f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468804} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3eda578-a166-50a0-a055-3491f285c249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468168} {:answer/id #uuid "162f9645-e89a-5701-ad26-d9ed38cd8047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "162f9645-e89a-5701-ad26-d9ed38cd8047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468169} :answer-field/id #uuid "c285d321-3810-5e14-84d2-43bf04bf70f6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468805} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b017c3c-7510-5e3d-b988-1b2e3d35ce99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468170} {:answer/id #uuid "178b845d-847d-59e6-9f16-2bed3653076e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b017c3c-7510-5e3d-b988-1b2e3d35ce99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468170} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6cf479-04ac-5307-9b8b-3196833e508d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a5c031b-68ff-5d30-80f0-79dec42f9067" :question/math-academy-id "q-75729" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=2v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=2{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{2}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{1}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819 :topic/id #uuid "c29ac9c3-f2c1-572b-9251-f82101c5a30e" :topic/math-academy-id 1375 :topic/title "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa80f00-8548-5f74-b6fb-5dbccba58f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451645} {:answer/id #uuid "b3040f07-0197-5026-ab47-91dd85bce65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3040f07-0197-5026-ab47-91dd85bce65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451646} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d2b06d5-adbe-536a-a0fa-c5c41b302478" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452670} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3228962-8156-5d0f-97a3-009bbc637e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451647} {:answer/id #uuid "07258935-c7b9-559d-82e2-b673b9b5d690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07258935-c7b9-559d-82e2-b673b9b5d690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451648} :answer-field/id #uuid "4854e4f1-f53c-547d-b393-efd9f0eccb7e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452671} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e09fd43d-de8c-5999-95be-1fd16231599c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451649} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0a7e6-a439-5558-9140-6a92f277aa45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0a7e6-a439-5558-9140-6a92f277aa45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451650} :answer-field/id #uuid "931bc191-690d-5796-9a1c-a924d4fb2c87" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb537936-d19a-5b11-8425-d86d338ccdb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-75730" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$\n\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{4}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{4}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $4$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=4v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(5v)=5(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=5(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048176 :topic/id #uuid "85bbc017-1b3b-59cb-935c-a883af5d0e12" :topic/math-academy-id 612 :topic/title "Continuity Over an Interval"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03abb9a7-2b48-52fb-a796-934e69dbf835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468172} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4cfef1-5371-5f3e-857e-64eebc7ac3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac4cfef1-5371-5f3e-857e-64eebc7ac3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468173} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6c909f-63c0-5135-bbf9-4d267137c73a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468807} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6aec76-6d7b-5744-8ce8-2ebf6affd726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468174} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ff5181-6f4d-55aa-a9ac-4242973df9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7ff5181-6f4d-55aa-a9ac-4242973df9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468175} :answer-field/id #uuid "a47d1666-97cf-5ba1-8e08-40775e23e294" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1035b0df-44b2-5fe0-afef-c28f18fd27c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468176} {:answer/id #uuid "61af2a66-29c7-569c-b2ed-0c194422b56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61af2a66-29c7-569c-b2ed-0c194422b56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468177} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4acdf0c-ddba-5b17-9a40-e4b3170b2b16" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca01d454-a476-5d37-9896-aa972462aa23" :question/math-academy-id "q-76208" :question/problem "![](images/ab/abfd15c8ed134a30ad719f6a857268427511e7f1f15ecfe0d27e5026b5401a83.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)$\n- $x\\in [-2,3)$\n- $x\\in [3,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on the open interval $(-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-2,3).$ It is continuous on $(-2,3)$ and also right continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[3,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(3,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048176 :topic/id #uuid "85bbc017-1b3b-59cb-935c-a883af5d0e12" :topic/math-academy-id 612 :topic/title "Continuity Over an Interval"}]}] :question/answ