f 17592186468183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f51d1c0f-41ac-5c83-95c2-57eacf511cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468182} :answer-field/id #uuid "13930865-bec4-50ea-80b9-6dc27b984457" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d16a77c7-8df1-5b6e-bc46-f6c511c4b268" :question/math-academy-id "q-76210" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e787dda0cccdafaa6a93176daea5f954b1cd1da0beaff410a6563536459208e.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$\n- $x\\in [-2,0]$\n- $x\\in [-1,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and also left continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-2,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-1,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(-1,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048176 :topic/id #uuid "85bbc017-1b3b-59cb-935c-a883af5d0e12" :topic/math-academy-id 612 :topic/title "Continuity Over an Interval"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:a